<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Kaffa</h1><p class="st-meta">1390 CE – 1897 CE (approx.) · capital Bonga</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A forested kingdom of south-western Ethiopia, often linked with the origin of coffee, that kept its independence until 1897.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>According to tradition the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/theodosia">Kaffa</a> was founded around 1390 by the Minjo dynasty, which united the Kafa-speaking clans of the highland forests south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gojeb-river">Gojeb</a> river. It later absorbed the remnants of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-ennarea">Kingdom of Ennarea</a>, whose rulers fled south in the 18th century, and expanded over neighbouring peoples.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The king, or tato, was a sacred ruler secluded from his subjects and advised by a council of seven great officials. The kingdom was defended by deep ditches and fortified gates. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/theodosia">Kaffa</a> exported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, civet, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> and slaves, and its religion blended traditional beliefs with Christian elements. The highland province gave its name, by some accounts, to coffee.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1897 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II's</a> armies conquered <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/theodosia">Kaffa</a> after a long resistance, and its last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaki-sherocho">Gaki Sherocho</a>, was taken captive. War, famine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slave-raiding">slave raiding</a> devastated the population, and the kingdom became a province of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kafa-zone">Kafa Zone</a> preserves the name.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-kaffa.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Kaffa" width="320" height="231"><figcaption>Image: Antoine d'Abbadie · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antoine_d%27Abbadie_-_Vue_11_b_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1390 CE – 1897 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bonga</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gaki Sherocho</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kafa</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kaffa, Kafa, Kingdom of Kafa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1644&amp;focus=kingdom-of-kaffa" aria-label="Kingdom of Kaffa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5683 1906 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Kaffa lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6003 2162L6029 1964"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6029 1964h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6003 2162h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6003 2162h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1644&amp;focus=kingdom-of-kaffa">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

